{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== SESSION: e369 Jira DC — the certification trap ruled on and fixed; five children in review; nothing landable ===\n\n--- THE BLOCKER THAT DOMINATES EVERYTHING ---\n`src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py` is **803 LOC on gerrit/main**, over the LOCKED 800 cap.\nThe module-size gate scans every `src/rebar` file, so **no change in the repo can earn `Verified +1`**\nuntil it is split. I did not touch that file's size; it crossed the cap in `339262b990 (af11)`. The\noperator confirmed another session owns the split. Everything below is pushed and waiting on it.\n\nSecond, separate CI failure, and it WAS mine: the **comment-hygiene gate**. A bare CI run id in a\ndocstring is a rot-prone reference; the guard wants a resolvable ticket id. My fix for it landed in a\nLATER commit of the stack, so changes 1370/1371 kept failing while 1372+ passed. **Gerrit tests each\npatchset independently — verifying only the stack tip proves nothing about its ancestors.** Rebuilt\nthe stack so the fix sits in the commit that introduced the violation, then verified hygiene + ruff\non EACH of the five commits separately.\n\n--- MERGED (2) ---\n  1361  1019  clamp max_tokens to the model being called   (restored the verifier's sight)\n  1359  4b9e  the Epic Link clear probe\n\n--- IN REVIEW (8), all blocked on the module-size gate ---\n  1370 d582  collision-free rekey target        1378 738a  attest a disposition close\n  1371 275e  non-admin application-properties   1380 2bc7  guard 2 shared-key loaders\n  1372 59b2  let the vacuous assertions fail    1381 2e47  assert issue-type names unambiguous\n  1373 4b9e  keep probe evidence on setup fail  1375 2e47  vocabulary decisions as data\n\n--- THE CERTIFICATION TRAP: two correct rules, no honest exit ---\nThe close path exempts a `duplicate`/`not_a_bug`/`escalated` bug naming a live replacement from\ncompletion verification — right, since it never claimed to implement its own ACs. But it returned no\nsign signal, and `child_closure_findings` counts ANY unsigned closure as uncertified, withholding the\nparent's signature. Reopening took the same exemption every time; `--force-close` produced exactly\nthe state the ruling forbids.\n\n**The exemption keyed on the link that DOCUMENTS the disposition:**\n\n    disposition closes WITH a live replacement link  ->  7 of 7 UNSIGNED\n    disposition closes WITHOUT one                   ->  7 CERTIFIED\n\nSo certification was reachable only for duplicates that HID their replacement. Operator ruled: attest\nthem. Implemented (1378) — deterministic, no LLM call, and the manifest says\n`completion-verifier: DISPOSITION duplicate` + the replacement id rather than a `PASS` that never\nhappened. Verified end to end: 0059 is now `certified`, and e369's uncertified count fell 2 -> 1.\n\n--- WHO FORCED AND WHO DID NOT: the earlier reopen conflated two tickets ---\nThe reopen rationale said 0059 and 2bc7 both \"closed WITHOUT a completion-verifier attestation … so\nit never passed the gate\". **True for 2bc7, FALSE for 0059.**\n\n    0059  FORCE_CLOSE records: 0   class=duplicate     took the sanctioned exemption\n    2bc7  FORCE_CLOSE records: 1   class=preexisting   an agent bypassing the gate\n\n`preexisting` is not a disposition class, so 2bc7 genuinely owed verification, could not satisfy the\nreferencing-commit precheck (its fix landed under J4's commit naming 0a94), and was forced.\n\n**I first inferred \"not forced\" from the STATUS event lacking a force field. That was wrong** —\nforce-close is recorded as an audit COMMENT, never in the event, so a forced and an unforced close\nleave identical event keys. The comment is the only discriminator.\n\n--- METHOD: three scans lied to me before one told the truth ---\n1. **A regex scan reported \"109 unguarded loaders\". Artifact.** My function-body regex matched\n   `(.*?)\\):` for the arg list, which mis-parses every `def f(...) -> Type:`. The AST pass gave 4 of\n   213 cache-first. I discarded the regex number rather than reporting it.\n2. **Two force-close scans returned ZERO** — one pointed at a tracker copy lacking the records, one\n   used `subprocess` `grep`, which disagreed with the shell's (193 files vs 0). Only pure Python was\n   trustworthy. An empty result is not evidence of absence until the scan itself is checked.\n3. **I published an audit table with a wrong row** and corrected it: `test_mutation.py` does not\n   overwrite `sys.modules`, it returns an UNREGISTERED private copy — which is literally 2bc7's\n   title, so that helper is one cross-file `isinstance` from reproducing the bug. Safe by\n   circumstance, not construction.\n\n--- THE LABEL-LIMIT TRAP THE EPIC'S OWN ORACLES CAUGHT ---\nDC rejects a 255-char label and accepts 254, so I tightened the shared constant. **Four\ncharacterization oracles went red.** `sanitize_label` RAISES above the ceiling rather than\ntruncating, so a shared 254 makes the live-validated CLOUD path reject a label Cloud accepts —\ntrading a silent DC defect for a loud Cloud regression, the exact risk this epic was built to avoid.\nReverted; the fix is a per-deployment ceiling on 049e's `comment_max_chars` precedent. The oracles\ndid their job on me.\n\n--- THE PROBE ANSWERED NOTHING, AND DESTROYED THE EVIDENCE OF WHY ---\nRun 30930839323: `PROBE SETUP FAILED: Epic Link=None Epic Name=None`, then\n`No files were found with the provided path`. Evidence was written only after the LAST question while\nevery setup-failure path raises `SystemExit` — so the one artifact built to explain a failure was\nguaranteed to be discarded exactly when needed. On an amd64-only image each attempt costs a full boot,\nwhich makes an undiagnosable failure the expensive kind. Fixed (1373). **4b9e's blocking unknown is\nstill OPEN and the Rank 1 cell stays unwritten.**\n\nConstraint 1 refined: a `workflow_dispatch` file must exist on the DEFAULT branch to be dispatchable,\nbut once it does, `--ref` may target any ref — so the landing cost is per-workflow, not per-iteration.\n\n--- FILED THIS SESSION ---\n  738a  the certification trap (FIXED, 1378)\n  502b  6 tickets carry a FORCE_CLOSE record AND a certified attestation — OBSERVATION, not diagnosed;\n        the trusted op-cert gate service is the leading unverified hypothesis. `e95e` is signed BEFORE\n        its force-close, which that hypothesis does not explain.\n  e351  209 of 213 by-path loaders are not cache-first, on keys shared by up to 9 files\n\n--- STATE HANDED OVER ---\n * epic e369 in_progress, claimed. **AC4 still UNTICKED** — the live harness run at current main\n   (30933704255) is the evidence, dispatched per the operator's restatement that real DC sync\n   demonstrated by the live CI environment is the load-bearing requirement.\n * e369 uncertified: 1 (b586, `stale-material` — remediable by re-closing).\n * 0059 CLOSED + certified. 2bc7 / 2e47 / 275e / 59b2 / d582 still OPEN — deliberately: their changes\n   are not `Verified +1`, and 2e47 has two genuinely unmet ACs (the per-deployment label ceiling and\n   the duplicate-`Task` resolution).\n * Nothing closed to unblock the epic. Every close waits on green CI."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785866257465776001,"uuid":"1984c0dd-e2f0-434c-880d-6efd0cd7752d"}